ence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|=\\frac{1}{5}<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442196 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442206 1069 "q-141402" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442206 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n4,\\,4,\\,4,\\,4,\\,…\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442206 1072 #uuid "affca196-0a76-5ce7-a774-751bad763eba" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442206 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe  given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $r=1,$ i.e., the sequence converges and converges to $4.$" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442191 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442207 1069 "q-141416" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442207 1071 "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{n-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442207 1072 #uuid "b1e6aead-9808-569e-a808-627f1ba618d2" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442207 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{n-1}$ is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=-1.$ \n\nSince $r=-1,$ the sequence diverges." 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442188 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442208 1069 "q-141392" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442208 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n-3,\\,6,\\,-12,\\,24,\\,…\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442208 1072 #uuid "ecbf1f26-9acd-560e-9d67-c86b72436e88" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442208 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe  given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6}{(-3)}=-2.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|=2>1,$ the sequence diverges." 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442189 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442209 1069 "q-74622" 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17592186442209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534874 17592186045417 true] [17